  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". I give two replies. Reply A is:\n> ```javascript\n> {\"c\":3}\n> ```\n> And here is another object:\n> {\"a\":1}\nReply B is:\n> ```json\n> {\"c\":3}\n> ```\n> And here is another object:\n> {\"a\":1}\nThe only difference is the fence label: javascript versus json. Under the rule, both replies have a code fence holding an object, and that fenced object is {\"c\":3}; the trailing object {\"a\":1} is not inside a fence. Therefore the rule requires the same answer for both: the object scored is {\"c\":3}. The function, however, as its code shows, only recognises fences labelled json, JSON, or unlabelled; for Reply A's javascript fence it finds no fenced candidate and falls back to the last top-level object, which is {\"a\":1}, while for Reply B it returns {\"c\":3}. So the function tells the two replies apart where the rule says it should not. This is the weaker kind of failure, not the original find of a pair the check answers the same on. I would be wrong if the function returns the same answer for both replies, for example if it returns {\"c\":3} for Reply A as well, because then it would not tell them apart.",
  "commitments": "The function returns {\"a\":1} for Reply A and {\"c\":3} for Reply B."
